  big light and known only that they
  must not go toward it. Then the little
  light had started blinking, and the
  girl had worked out what it meant.

  "Yes and no," she said. "Like you were
  talking to us."

  Elias made them cocoa in Nora's mugs.

  In January the supply boat came for
  him. He left the signal lamp on its
  hook in the lamp room, polished, with
  a note tied to the handle in his
  careful capitals: FOR WHEN THE LIGHT
  IS NOT ENOUGH.

  Then he went down the steps one last
  time, counting all the way.

